+# Pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes how members are reminded of a pickup, or proposes moving reminders from SMS to email. The day
+before a pickup slot, larder texts the member that their pantry order is ready, and this page says why the reminder
+goes by SMS although email would be free. Written after [issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md), the third proposal in a
+year to switch to email, when the reasons lived only in the memory of the volunteers who had tried it.
+
+## What a member receives
+
+The day before the slot, one SMS to the member's phone:
+
+> Your pantry order is ready for pickup {pickup}.
+
+`larder/reminders.py` sends it; its `CHANNEL` constant picks SMS or email.
+
+## Why SMS, not email
+
+SMS costs the pantry about 40 euros a month, and email would cost nothing, so from April to June 2025 the pantry sent
+reminders by email instead:
+
+- The email provider reported that 34% of the reminder emails were never opened.
+- Missed pickups rose from 6% of pantry orders to 13%. Each missed pickup is a bag already packed, and its fresh food
+  goes in the bin by closing time.
+- Many members share one phone across the family and never check email; an SMS they see.
+
+Reminders went back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back to 6% by August. When issue 52 raised the
+cost again, the maintainers kept SMS.
